{"qid":"Q466433","names":["Karkota Dynasty"],"title":"Karkota Dynasty","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Karkota dynasty ruled the Kashmir valley from approximately 625 CE, founded by Durlabhavardhan, whose son Chandrapida (r. c. 712–720) resisted Tang Chinese imperial pressure and successfully sought recognition from the Tang court as an equal. The dynasty's apex came under Lalitaditya Muktapida (r. c. 724–760), whose campaigns ranged across northern India, Central Asia, and as far as Kanauj, making him the most expansionist Kashmiri ruler in recorded history; his patronage produced the sun temple at Martand (c. 725–756), the finest surviving early medieval temple complex in Kashmir. The Karkota line was supplanted by the Utpala dynasty under Avantivarman in 855 CE following a period of palace coups and administrative collapse.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Karkota core territory in the Kashmir valley is attested by the Rajatarangini chronicle of Kalhana (composed 1148–1149); claims of wider empire beyond the valley are treated with scholarly caution as they may be literary exaggeration.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Karkota dynasty","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karkota_dynasty"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Lalitaditya Muktapida","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lalitaditya_Muktapida"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Martand Sun Temple","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Martand_Sun_Temple"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Karkota dynasty","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Durlabhaka Pratapaditya I","nativeName":"दुर्लभक प्रतापादित्य I","roman":"Durlabhaka Pratāpāditya I","start"